WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:07.440
Learning how to identify possible data&nbsp;
is a fundamental skill if you're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:11.440
to work with data. You have to&nbsp;
see data when it isn't obvious.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:18.080
Data is truly everywhere and it is almost always&nbsp;
more than what you initially think. Let's start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:23.200
with this simple example of a chair. Think&nbsp;
of what data you see when you see this chair.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:30.480
Did you notice that it has a color? What about&nbsp;
the material? And, what about it being furniture?

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:36.000
If we dig deeper for even more data, you will&nbsp;
see that the chair has dimensions like height&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:42.560
and weight. These things impact our shipping and&nbsp;
the cost of shipping. That takes us even further,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:49.040
like suppliers. Where are the locations of&nbsp;
those suppliers? How many suppliers do we have?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:52.240
And, how many chairs do they have in stock?

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:56.400
What about all the dates related to&nbsp;
the chair, like when it's ordered?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:03.520
When it was shipped? When it's delivered?&nbsp;
Again, identifying the not-so-obvious data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:07.120
can help you in a million different&nbsp;
ways as you start your analyst career.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:12.400
Try your data lens out on the items that you&nbsp;
work with and see how much data you identify.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:17.360
This will help you to ask better questions&nbsp;
in almost every data-related project.

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:18.320
Learning about data fields and types

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:24.160
It's important to understand what data looks like.&nbsp;
Most people will think about the values of data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:31.520
But, to an analyst, the data looks like data&nbsp;
types, fields and values. A data type defines what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:37.840
type of data goes into a field. A date data type&nbsp;
will contain only dates, numbers only numbers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:42.320
It also controls what you can do with&nbsp;
the data values of that data type.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:48.320
For example, if I need to add 30 days, then I'll&nbsp;
want to be sure I'm working with a date data type.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:53.840
Data has so many types, and each program has&nbsp;
different ways to handle and format them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:56.640
Now let's get started with the basics.

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:03.440
Common data types are text or string, date,&nbsp;
date and time and number. When we first look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:08.240
at data on any data set, it's typically the&nbsp;
field headings and the values that we see.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:13.920
But as a data analyst, remember you must dig&nbsp;
deeper to see what types are behind that data.

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:21.440
Here's an example. You see both the field heading&nbsp;
and the value. The field name is FirstName, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:27.520
the field value is Robin. Field headings and all&nbsp;
the values then go on to make up records or record&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:34.080
sets. This probably looks familiar to you. The&nbsp;
spreadsheets you encounter are probably structured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:39.920
with records like this already. This gives us a&nbsp;
lot of flexibility when working with data tools.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:45.200
Depending on the data you encounter, it might&nbsp;
be your first step to get it into a record&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:53.440
set format. Data tools you will work with&nbsp;
will expect the data in this record format.

